        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-inner-product-space",
          "carrier": "items/def-inner-product-space.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $F$ be either $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, with conjugation equal to the identity on $\\mathbb R$ and with complex conjugation as in [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]. An **inner product** on an $F$-vector space $V$ is a function $\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,\\,\\cdot\\,\\rangle:V\\times V\\to F$ such that for all $u,v,w\\in V$ and $a,b\\in F$:\n\n1. $\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle=a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle$;\n2. $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle v,u\\rangle}$;\n3. $\\langle v,v\\rangle$ is real and nonnegative, and $\\langle v,v\\rangle=0$ if and only if $v=0$.\n\nThe first two clauses imply conjugate-linearity in the second argument: $\\langle u,av+bw\\rangle=\\overline a\\langle u,v\\rangle+\\overline b\\langle u,w\\rangle$. This is the linear-first convention of [[def-sesquilinear-and-hermitian-forms-over-a-field-with-involution]]. A vector space equipped with an inner product is an **inner product space**.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [[def-inner-product-space]], [[thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases]]:\nthe standard tensor words form a finite basis of $H_N$; for $N=0$ the empty\ntensor is the one-element basis. Declaring those words orthonormal gives the\nstandard tensor-product inner product, linear in its first argument; more\ngenerally, elementary tensors from finite bases form a basis of their\nalgebraic tensor product.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.2, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring. If $M$ is free with basis $(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $N$ is free with basis $(f_j)_{j\\in J}$, then $M\\otimes_RN$ is free with basis\n\n$$\\bigl(e_i\\otimes f_j\\bigr)_{(i,j)\\in I\\times J}.$$\n\nEquivalently, the canonical map $R^{(I\\times J)}\\to M\\otimes_RN$ sending the standard basis vector at $(i,j)$ to $e_i\\otimes f_j$ is an isomorphism. This includes an empty basis in either factor.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [[def-inner-product-space]], [[thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases]]:\nthe standard tensor words form a finite basis of $H_N$; for $N=0$ the empty\ntensor is the one-element basis. Declaring those words orthonormal gives the\nstandard tensor-product inner product, linear in its first argument; more\ngenerally, elementary tensors from finite bases form a basis of their\nalgebraic tensor product.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.2, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $V,W$ be inner product spaces (Real and complex inner product spaces, with the inner product linear in the first argument) over the same field and let $T:V\\to W$ be linear (Linear map between vector spaces over the same field). An **adjoint** of $T$ is a linear map $T^*:W\\to V$ such that\n\n$$\\langle Tv,w\\rangle_W=\\langle v,T^*w\\rangle_V$$\n\nfor every $v\\in V$ and $w\\in W$. Existence is not part of the definition; in finite dimensions it will follow from Riesz representation.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F7] [[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]]: for a\nlinear map $T$ between finite-dimensional inner-product spaces, its adjoint\n$T^*$ satisfies $\\langle Tv,w\\rangle=\\langle v,T^*w\\rangle$; the inner\nproducts here are linear in the first argument.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.2, 2.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-adjoint-exists-and-is-unique-in-finite-dimension",
          "carrier": "items/thm-adjoint-exists-and-is-unique-in-finite-dimension.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Every linear map $T:V\\to W$ between finite-dimensional real or complex inner product spaces has a unique adjoint $T^*:W\\to V$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F10] [[thm-adjoint-exists-and-is-unique-in-finite-dimension]]: every linear\nmap between finite-dimensional inner-product spaces has a unique adjoint.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F10 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition",
          "carrier": "items/thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "If $W$ is a subspace of a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space $V$, then\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp.$$\n\nThus every $v\\in V$ has unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F8] [[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]: every subspace of\na finite-dimensional inner-product space has an orthogonal complement, and\nthe space is their direct sum.",
