    "lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-configuration-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Give $\\mathbb R^4$ its Euclidean topology, whose basic open sets are the\nEuclidean open balls, and write a point as an ordered pair $(x,y)$ with\n$x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$. The **ordered configuration space** is the subspace\n$$F_2(\\mathbb R^2):=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2:x\\ne y\\}$$\nwith the subspace topology. The transposition acts by $(x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)$.\nThe **unordered configuration space of two distinct planar points** is\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2):=F_2(\\mathbb R^2)/\\bigl((x,y)\\sim(y,x)\\bigr)$$\nwith the quotient topology of the canonical projection\n$q:F_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\to C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. We write its points as unordered\nsets $\\{x,y\\}$; equality in this set means equality up to transposition.\nThe diagonal $x=y$ is excluded, so collisions are not points of this space.\n\nThe topology on each finite Cartesian coordinate space used below is its\nEuclidean topology; on a finite product it is equivalently the topology with\nbasis the products of open sets in the factors.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a topological space and let $x_0\\in X$. A **based loop at $x_0$**\nis a path $\\alpha:I\\to X$ with $\\alpha(0)=x_0=\\alpha(1)$\n([[def-path-connected]]). Two based loops are equivalent when they are\npath-homotopic relative to the endpoints. This is an equivalence relation by\n[[cor-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy-are-equivalence-relations]].\n\nThe **fundamental group set** of $X$ at $x_0$ is\n\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0):=\\{\\,[\\alpha]:\\alpha\\text{ is a based loop at }x_0\\,\\}.$$\n\nFor composable paths, write\n\n$$(\\alpha*\\beta)(s):=\\begin{cases}\\alpha(2s),&0\\leq s\\leq\\tfrac12,\\\\ \\beta(2s-1),&\\tfrac12\\leq s\\leq1.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe finite closed-pasting argument already carried out in\n[[def-path-connected]] shows that this is a path. The multiplication proposed\non loop classes is\n\n$$[\\alpha][\\beta]:=[\\alpha*\\beta].$$\n\n**Order convention.** The product $[\\alpha][\\beta]$ traverses $\\alpha$ first\nand $\\beta$ second. Every product on this page uses this convention.\n\nThe constant loop at $x_0$ is denoted $c_{x_0}$, and the reversed loop is\n$\\bar\\alpha(s):=\\alpha(1-s)$. The next theorem proves that multiplication is\nindependent of representatives and that $[c_{x_0}]$ and $[\\bar\\alpha]$ are the\nidentity and inverse required by the group axioms.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-induced-fundamental-group-map-functoriality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:(X,x_0)\\to(Y,y_0)$ be a continuous map with $f(x_0)=y_0$. Then\n\n$$f_*([\\alpha])=[f\\circ\\alpha]$$\n\nis a well-defined group homomorphism. For pointed continuous maps,\n\n$$\\operatorname{id}_*=\\operatorname{id},\\qquad (g\\circ f)_*=g_*\\circ f_*.$$\n\nIf $f_0,f_1:(X,x_0)\\to(Y,y_0)$ are homotopic through a homotopy that keeps\n$x_0$ at $y_0$, then $(f_0)_*=(f_1)_*$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fundamental-group-of-the-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\operatorname{Deg}:\\pi_1(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,[0])\\to(\\mathbb Z,+)$ is an isomorphism. Its inverse is\n\n$$n\\longmapsto[\\omega_n].$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the canonical copy of $\\mathbb Z$ inside $\\mathbb R$ fixed in\n[[lem-integer-part]]. For $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$, put\n\n$$x\\sim y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nThis is an equivalence relation. Indeed, $x-x=0\\in\\mathbb Z$; if\n$x-y\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $y-x=-(x-y)\\in\\mathbb Z$; and if\n$x-y,y-z\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)\\in\\mathbb Z$. The closure facts\nused here are part of the additive-group structure supplied by\n[[thm-int-comm-ring]], and the quotient-set construction is that of\n[[def-quotient-topology]].\n\nLet $[x]$ denote the equivalence class of $x$. Let\n$p:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the canonical projection,\n$p(x)=[x]$. Thus\n\n$$p(x)=p(y)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nLet $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ carry the quotient topology induced by $p$.\nThe circle is $S^1:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ with the quotient topology induced by $p(x)=[x]$ and basepoint $[0]$; moreover $p^{-1}([0])=\\mathbb Z$ and $p(x+n)=p(x)$ for every real $x$ and integer $n$.\n\nThe last assertions follow directly from the displayed fibre criterion:\n$p(x)=[0]$ exactly when $x\\in\\mathbb Z$, while\n$(x+n)-x=n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n\n$$C=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$$\n\nwith the Euclidean subspace topology. The function\n\n$$h:\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow C,\\qquad h([t])=(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t),$$\n\nis a homeomorphism. Thus $[t]\\mapsto(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t)$ is a homeomorphism from $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ to the unit circle and sends $[0]$ to $(1,0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-quotient-universal-property",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $q : X \\to Y$ be a quotient map ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then:\n\n1. **Characteristic property.** For every space $W$ and every function\n   $k : Y \\to W$,\n   $$k \\text{ is continuous } \\iff k \\circ q \\text{ is continuous} .$$\n2. **Factorisation.** Let $f : X \\to W$ be continuous and **constant on the\n   fibres of $q$**, that is $q(x) = q(x')$ implies $f(x) = f(x')$. Then there is\n   exactly one function $\\bar f : Y \\to W$ with $\\bar f \\circ q = f$, and it is\n   continuous.\n3. **Composites.** If $q : X \\to Y$ and $p : Y \\to Z$ are quotient maps then\n   $p \\circ q : X \\to Z$ is a quotient map.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For an ordered pair put $c=(x+y)/2$ and $z=(x-y)/2$. This is an\ninvertible linear coordinate change, with inverse $(c,z)\\mapsto(c+z,c-z)$;\nthe collision-free condition is $z\\ne0$. In polar coordinates write\n$z=ru$ with $r=\\|z\\|>0$ and $u\\in S^1$. These formulas and their inverses\nare continuous, so they give a homeomorphism\n$F_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times S^1$. Transposition\nfixes $c,r$ and sends $u$ to $-u$. If $A:=S^1/(u\\sim-u)$ has quotient\ntopology, the map $(c,r,u)\\mapsto(c,r,[u])$ is an open quotient map: the\ncircle quotient is open because the saturation of an open set is its union\nwith its antipodal image, and products of open rectangles show the same for\nthe product map. Its fibers are exactly the transposition orbits. Thus it\ninduces a homeomorphism\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong X:=\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times A.$$",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\eta:\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\to S^1$ be the homeomorphism\n$\\eta([t])=(\\cos2\\pi t,\\sin2\\pi t)$ from [F5]. The antipodal map on $S^1$\ncorresponds under $\\eta$ to $[t]\\mapsto[t+1/2]$. The continuous map\n$D_2:\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, $D_2([t]):=[2t]$,\nis well-defined and continuous by [F6] applied to $t\\mapsto[2t]$ on\n$\\mathbb R$; its fibres are exactly the pairs $[t],[t+1/2]$. Hence\n$\\psi:A\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, $\\psi([\\eta([t])]):=[2t]$, is well-defined\nand continuous by the quotient topology. Its inverse is\n$[s]\\mapsto[\\eta([s/2])]$; changing $s$ by an integer changes $s/2$ by a\nhalf-integer, which is the same class in $A$. This inverse is continuous by\n[F6] applied to the continuous map $s\\mapsto[\\eta([s/2])]$ on $\\mathbb R$;\nthe two formulas compose to the identity, so $\\psi$ is a homeomorphism.\nUnder the homeomorphism of step 1.1, $q_0$ is\n$(0,1,[(1,0)])$, which $\\psi$ sends to $[0]$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "construct",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On $X$ define $r(c,\\rho,a):=\\psi(a)$ and\n$i([t]):=(0,1,\\psi^{-1}([t]))$. These maps are continuous, preserve the\nspecified basepoints, and $r\\circ i$ is the identity. The maps\n$$H_s(c,\\rho,a):=((1-s)c,(1-s)\\rho+s,a),\\qquad 0\\leq s\\leq1,$$\nare a continuous based homotopy from $\\operatorname{id}_X$ to $i\\circ r$:\nthe radius is positive since $(1-s)\\rho+s>0$, and the basepoint\n$(0,1,[(1,0)])$ is fixed for every $s$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], $(r\\circ i)_*=r_*i_*=\\operatorname{id}$ and the based homotopy\nin step 3.1 gives $(i\\circ r)_*=i_*r_*=\\operatorname{id}$ on the based-loop\nclasses of [F2]. Thus $r_*$ and $i_*$ are inverse isomorphisms. The pointed\nhomeomorphism of step 1.1 transports this isomorphism back to\n$C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$; applying [F4] gives\n$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong\\mathbb Z$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the coordinates of step 1.1, $\\gamma$ keeps $c=0$ and $r=1$ and has\n$u(t)=(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t)$. Its image under $r$ is $[t]=\\omega_1(t)$,\nwhich is the positive generator by [F4]. Replacing $\\pi t$ by $2\\pi t$\ndescribes a full turn of the relative direction; its image is $[2t]=\\omega_2(t)$,\nso its class is twice $[\\gamma]$. This identifies exchange and winding\norientation without using a diagram.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
