          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $q : X \\to Y$ be a quotient map ([[def-quotient-topology]]). Then:\n\n1. **Characteristic property.** For every space $W$ and every function\n   $k : Y \\to W$,\n   $$k \\text{ is continuous } \\iff k \\circ q \\text{ is continuous} .$$\n2. **Factorisation.** Let $f : X \\to W$ be continuous and **constant on the\n   fibres of $q$**, that is $q(x) = q(x')$ implies $f(x) = f(x')$. Then there is\n   exactly one function $\\bar f : Y \\to W$ with $\\bar f \\circ q = f$, and it is\n   continuous.\n3. **Composites.** If $q : X \\to Y$ and $p : Y \\to Z$ are quotient maps then\n   $p \\circ q : X \\to Z$ is a quotient map.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [[thm-quotient-universal-property]]: a continuous map constant on the\nfibres of a quotient map factors uniquely as a continuous map out of the\nquotient.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.2."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "The exchange path γ(t) at t=0 and t=1.",
          "result": "Both endpoints are q₀; its normalized angle lift runs from 0 to 1 and gives the positive generator.",
          "verifies": "Generator computation in step 5.1."
        },
        {
          "check": "A full relative turn with angle 2πt.",
          "result": "The quotient-circle coordinate is [2t], so the loop has class 2[γ].",
          "verifies": "Full relative-turn computation in step 5.1."
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-nrqmx-spin-half-temperley-lieb": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-spin-half-tensor-chain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\geq1$ be an integer. The finite spin-half tensor chain is the complex\ninner-product space\n$$\\mathcal H_N:=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$$\nwith the standard tensor-product inner product, linear in its first argument.\nFix the standard orthonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$ of $\\mathbb C^2$.\nLet $\\sigma^x,\\sigma^y,\\sigma^z$ be the Pauli matrices of the finite-spin\nmatrix supplier, and for $1\\leq k\\leq N$ define\n$$\\sigma_k^a:=I^{\\otimes(k-1)}\\otimes\\sigma^a\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-k)},\\qquad J_k^a:=\\tfrac12\\sigma_k^a\\quad(a\\in\\{x,y,z\\}).$$\nFor $1\\leq i<N$, define the adjacent swap $s_i$ on elementary tensors by\n$$s_i(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{i+1}\\otimes v_i\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N.$$\nWrite\n$$|\\omega\\rangle:=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(|0\\rangle\\otimes|1\\rangle-|1\\rangle\\otimes|0\\rangle),\\qquad P_i:=I^{\\otimes(i-1)}\\otimes P_\\omega\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-i-1)},$$\nwhere $P_\\omega v:=\\langle v,\\omega\\rangle\\,\\omega$ on the adjacent two\nfactors. Also put $e_i:=I_{\\mathcal H_N}-s_i$ for $1\\leq i<N$. If $N=1$,\nthese bond-indexed families are empty.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition of finite-dimensional tensor spaces and\noperators; it makes no physical claim about a material, a particle species, or\nan adopted statistics sector.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N=\\{0,\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\dots\\}$, put $N:=2s\\in\\mathbb N$ and let\n$V_s:=\\mathbb C^{N+1}$ carry the standard inner product\n$\\langle z,w\\rangle=\\sum_{k=0}^{N}z_k\\overline{w_k}$, linear in the first argument\n([[def-inner-product-space]]). Write $(e_m)_{-s\\le m\\le s}$ for the standard basis, indexed so that\n$e_m$ is the vector with entry $1$ in position $k=s-m$ and entry $0$ elsewhere; thus\n$\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=1$ if $m=n$ and $\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=0$ otherwise. Define $\\mathbb C$-linear maps\n$J_z,J_+,J_-\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V_s)$ by their values on this basis ([[def-linear-map]]):\n\n$$J_ze_m:=m\\,e_m,\\qquad J_+e_m:=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}\\,e_{m+1},\\qquad J_-e_m:=\\sqrt{(s+m)(s-m+1)}\\,e_{m-1},$$\n\nwhere $e_{s+1}:=0$ and $e_{-s-1}:=0$, and put\n\n$$J_x:=\\tfrac12(J_++J_-),\\qquad J_y:=\\tfrac1{2i}(J_+-J_-),\\qquad J^2:=J_x^2+J_y^2+J_z^2 .$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $J_z$, $J_x$ and $J_y$ are self-adjoint endomorphisms of $V_s$\n([[def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism]]), and $J_+^*=J_-$, $J_-^*=J_+$\n([[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]]);\n\n(b) on $V_s$ the commutators are\n\n$$[J_z,J_+]=J_+,\\qquad [J_z,J_-]=-J_-,\\qquad [J_+,J_-]=2J_z,\\qquad [J_x,J_y]=iJ_z,\\qquad [J_y,J_z]=iJ_x,\\qquad [J_z,J_x]=iJ_y ;$$\n\n(c) $J^2=J_-J_++J_z^2+J_z=J_+J_-+J_z^2-J_z=s(s+1)I$, and $[J^2,J_z]=[J^2,J_\\pm]=0$;\n\n(d) the matrix elements of the raising operator are\n$\\|J_+e_m\\|^2=(s-m)(s+m+1)$ and $\\|J_-e_m\\|^2=(s+m)(s-m+1)$, and\n$\\langle J_+e_m,e_{m+1}\\rangle=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}$, which is nonzero exactly when $m<s$;\n\n(e) for $s=\\tfrac12$, identify $V_{\\frac12}=\\mathbb C^2$ with the basis ordered as $(e_{\\frac12},e_{-\\frac12})$ and let\n\n$$\\sigma_x:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_y:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\ i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_z:=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nbe the Pauli matrices. Then $J_i=\\tfrac12\\sigma_i$ for $i=x,y,z$, $\\sigma_x^2=\\sigma_y^2=\\sigma_z^2=I$,\n$\\sigma_x\\sigma_y=i\\sigma_z$, $\\sigma_y\\sigma_z=i\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_z\\sigma_x=i\\sigma_y$, and consequently\n$\\sigma_i\\sigma_j+\\sigma_j\\sigma_i=2\\delta_{ij}I$ and $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I+i\\sum_k\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k$ for all $i,j$, where $\\delta_{ij}=1$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise, and $\\varepsilon_{ijk}=0$ when two of $i,j,k$ coincide, and otherwise $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$ is the sign of the permutation $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(i,j,k)$; the sum ranges over $k\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$. The same convention with indices $1,2,3$ uses the order-preserving relabelling $(1,2,3)=(x,y,z)$.\n\nAll statements are finite-dimensional linear algebra: no physical interpretation of $J_x,J_y,J_z$ as angular momentum, and no measurement postulate, is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-temperley-lieb-generator-presentation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit and $\\delta\\in R$. For each $n\\ge0$, let $F_n:=R\\langle u_1,\\ldots,u_{n-1}\\rangle$ be the free associative unital $R$-algebra on the indicated symbols ([[lem-hh-free-associative-ring-and-relations-descent]]). When $n=0$ or $n=1$, the symbol set is empty and $F_n=R$.\n\nLet $I_n$ be the two-sided ideal generated by\n\n$$u_i^2-\\delta u_i\\quad(1\\le i<n),\\qquad u_i u_j-u_j u_i\\quad(|i-j|>1),$$\n\nand\n\n$$u_i u_{i+1}u_i-u_i,\\qquad u_{i+1}u_i u_{i+1}-u_{i+1}\\quad(1\\le i\\le n-2).$$\n\nThe abstract **rank-$n$ Temperley–Lieb algebra** is the presented $R$-algebra\n\n$$P_n(R,\\delta):=F_n/I_n.$$\n\nThus its defining relations are $u_i^2=\\delta u_i$, $u_i u_{i\\pm1}u_i=u_i$, and $u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ for $|i-j|>1$. The definition of a presented $R$-algebra and its universal property are those of [[lem-hh-free-associative-ring-and-relations-descent]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-temperley-lieb-star-involution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For each integer $N\\geq0$, let $P_N(\\mathbb C,2)$ be the quotient of the free\nunital associative complex algebra on $u_1,\\ldots,u_{N-1}$ by the relations\n$$u_i^2=2u_i,\\qquad u_iu_j=u_ju_i\\ (|i-j|>1),\\qquad u_iu_{i+1}u_i=u_i,\\quad u_{i+1}u_iu_{i+1}=u_{i+1}.$$\nThis complex Temperley–Lieb algebra has a\nconjugate-linear anti-involution $*$ determined by\n$$c^*=\\overline c\\quad(c\\in\\mathbb C),\\qquad u_i^*=u_i\\quad(1\\leq i<N),\\qquad (xy)^*=y^*x^*.$$\nFor $N=0$ or $N=1$ this is complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers, let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with its standard\nHermitian inner product, and give $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$ its standard\ntensor-product inner product. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\}$ use the diagonal action\n$\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$. For a partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most\n$d$ rows, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding complex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N),\\qquad d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nEquip $S^\\lambda$ with any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip\n$M_\\lambda$ with the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On $\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2$, direct application to the four basis\nvectors gives\n$$s=\\tfrac12\\left(I+\\sigma_x\\otimes\\sigma_x+\\sigma_y\\otimes\\sigma_y+ \\sigma_z\\otimes\\sigma_z\\right).$$\nFor $|00\\rangle$ and $|11\\rangle$, the sum of the three Pauli products is\nthe vector itself; for $|01\\rangle$ it is $2|10\\rangle-|01\\rangle$, and\nfor $|10\\rangle$ it is $2|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle$, so the displayed operator\nis the factor swap in all four cases. The symmetric three-dimensional\nsubspace and antisymmetric line are an orthogonal decomposition; $s$ is $+I$\non the former and $-I$ on the latter, whose unit vector is $\\omega$.\nTherefore $I-s=2P_\\omega$, and $(I-s)^2=4P_\\omega^2=4P_\\omega=2(I-s)$.\nTensoring with identities gives $e_i=2P_i$, self-adjointness, and\n$e_i^2=2e_i$ for every bond.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On three adjacent factors, $P_{12}$ has image spanned by\n$|\\omega\\rangle_{12}\\otimes|0\\rangle$ and\n$|\\omega\\rangle_{12}\\otimes|1\\rangle$. Applying $P_{23}$ and then $P_{12}$\nto these two vectors gives, respectively,\n$$P_{12}P_{23}P_{12}(|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|0\\rangle_3) =\\tfrac14|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|0\\rangle_3,\\qquad P_{12}P_{23}P_{12}(|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|1\\rangle_3) =\\tfrac14|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|1\\rangle_3.$$\nFor example, the first intermediate vector is\n$P_{23}(|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|0\\rangle_3)=(-|001\\rangle+|010\\rangle)/(2\\sqrt2)$;\n$P_{12}$ kills $|001\\rangle$ and sends $|010\\rangle$ to\n$|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|0\\rangle_3/\\sqrt2$. The second computation is the\nexplicit calculation\n$P_{23}(|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|1\\rangle_3)=(-|101\\rangle+|110\\rangle)/(2\\sqrt2)$;\n$P_{12}$ kills $|110\\rangle$ and sends $|101\\rangle$ to\n$-|\\omega\\rangle_{12}|1\\rangle_3/\\sqrt2$, again giving the coefficient\n$1/4$. Since\n$P_{12}P_{23}P_{12}$ first projects into the displayed image, it follows on\nall eight basis vectors that $P_{12}P_{23}P_{12}=P_{12}/4$. Reversing the\nthree tensor factors exchanges $P_{12}$ and $P_{23}$ (the singlet line is\nunchanged by reversing its two entries), so also\n$P_{23}P_{12}P_{23}=P_{23}/4$. Hence\n$e_i e_{i+1}e_i=8P_iP_{i+1}P_i=2P_i=e_i$ and the reversed relation holds.\nOperators on disjoint tensor factors commute, giving the distant relation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The relations proved in steps 1.1 and 2.1 and [F3] give an algebra\nhomomorphism from the presented quotient, since every defining relation has\nzero image. Each $e_i$ is self-adjoint by step 1.1, so the operator adjoint\nof a word reverses its factors and fixes its generator images; conjugating\ncoefficients agrees with the algebra involution [F4]. Thus the representation\npreserves $*$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The adjacent swap is the place action of the transposition\n$(i,i+1)$, since that transposition is its own inverse in the left-place\nconvention. Therefore $e_i=I-\\rho((i,i+1))$. Applying the factor placement in\n[F5] shows that on each summand $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ it is\n$\\bigl(I-\\rho_\\lambda((i,i+1))\\bigr)\\otimes I_{M_\\lambda}$, as claimed.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The Statement specifies the N=1 empty-generator case; steps 3.1–4.1 give the resulting vacuous representation and factor action."
