      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-spin-half-tensor-chain",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-spin-half-tensor-chain.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $N\\geq1$ be an integer. The finite spin-half tensor chain is the complex\ninner-product space\n$$\\mathcal H_N:=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$$\nwith the standard tensor-product inner product, linear in its first argument.\nFix the standard orthonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$ of $\\mathbb C^2$.\nLet $\\sigma^x,\\sigma^y,\\sigma^z$ be the Pauli matrices of the finite-spin\nmatrix supplier, and for $1\\leq k\\leq N$ define\n$$\\sigma_k^a:=I^{\\otimes(k-1)}\\otimes\\sigma^a\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-k)},\\qquad J_k^a:=\\tfrac12\\sigma_k^a\\quad(a\\in\\{x,y,z\\}).$$\nFor $1\\leq i<N$, define the adjacent swap $s_i$ on elementary tensors by\n$$s_i(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{i+1}\\otimes v_i\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N.$$\nWrite\n$$|\\omega\\rangle:=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(|0\\rangle\\otimes|1\\rangle-|1\\rangle\\otimes|0\\rangle),\\qquad P_i:=I^{\\otimes(i-1)}\\otimes P_\\omega\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-i-1)},$$\nwhere $P_\\omega v:=\\langle v,\\omega\\rangle\\,\\omega$ on the adjacent two\nfactors. Also put $e_i:=I_{\\mathcal H_N}-s_i$ for $1\\leq i<N$. If $N=1$,\nthese bond-indexed families are empty.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition of finite-dimensional tensor spaces and\noperators; it makes no physical claim about a material, a particle species, or\nan adopted statistics sector.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [[def-nrqmx-spin-half-tensor-chain]]: $s_i$ swaps factors $i,i+1$,\n$|\\omega\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$,\n$P_i=I^{\\otimes(i-1)}\\otimes P_\\omega\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-i-1)}$,\nand $e_i=I-s_i$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft definition and exact interface inspected; proof is not applicable.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 supplies this interface in step(s) the stated assumptions."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-temperley-lieb-generator-presentation",
          "carrier": "items/def-temperley-lieb-generator-presentation.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit and $\\delta\\in R$. For each $n\\ge0$, let $F_n:=R\\langle u_1,\\ldots,u_{n-1}\\rangle$ be the free associative unital $R$-algebra on the indicated symbols ([[lem-hh-free-associative-ring-and-relations-descent]]). When $n=0$ or $n=1$, the symbol set is empty and $F_n=R$.\n\nLet $I_n$ be the two-sided ideal generated by\n\n$$u_i^2-\\delta u_i\\quad(1\\le i<n),\\qquad u_i u_j-u_j u_i\\quad(|i-j|>1),$$\n\nand\n\n$$u_i u_{i+1}u_i-u_i,\\qquad u_{i+1}u_i u_{i+1}-u_{i+1}\\quad(1\\le i\\le n-2).$$\n\nThe abstract **rank-$n$ Temperley–Lieb algebra** is the presented $R$-algebra\n\n$$P_n(R,\\delta):=F_n/I_n.$$\n\nThus its defining relations are $u_i^2=\\delta u_i$, $u_i u_{i\\pm1}u_i=u_i$, and $u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ for $|i-j|>1$. The definition of a presented $R$-algebra and its universal property are those of [[lem-hh-free-associative-ring-and-relations-descent]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [[def-temperley-lieb-generator-presentation]]: $P_N(\\mathbb C,2)$ is\ndefined by $u_i^2=2u_i$, $u_i u_{i\\pm1}u_i=u_i$, and\n$u_i u_j=u_j u_i$ for $|i-j|>1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqmx-temperley-lieb-star-involution",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqmx-temperley-lieb-star-involution.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For each integer $N\\geq0$, let $P_N(\\mathbb C,2)$ be the quotient of the free\nunital associative complex algebra on $u_1,\\ldots,u_{N-1}$ by the relations\n$$u_i^2=2u_i,\\qquad u_iu_j=u_ju_i\\ (|i-j|>1),\\qquad u_iu_{i+1}u_i=u_i,\\quad u_{i+1}u_iu_{i+1}=u_{i+1}.$$\nThis complex Temperley–Lieb algebra has a\nconjugate-linear anti-involution $*$ determined by\n$$c^*=\\overline c\\quad(c\\in\\mathbb C),\\qquad u_i^*=u_i\\quad(1\\leq i<N),\\qquad (xy)^*=y^*x^*.$$\nFor $N=0$ or $N=1$ this is complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [[lem-nrqmx-temperley-lieb-star-involution]]: the presented algebra has\nthe conjugate-linear anti-involution fixing every generator and reversing\nproducts.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft statement and complete proof inspected in this batch; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category",
          "carrier": "items/def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and let $\\delta\\in R$. The object labels are the nonnegative integers $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$. For labels $m,n$, place bottom points $b_1,\\ldots,b_m$ and top points $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$ along the rectangle from left to right on their respective edges. Their cyclic order is $t_1,\\ldots,t_n,b_m,\\ldots,b_1$. When $m=n=0$, the empty matching is included.\n\nAn $(m,n)$ **diagram** is a perfect matching of these $m+n$ boundary labels which is noncrossing: there are no four labels $a,b,c,d$ in cyclic order whose matched pairs are $\\{a,c\\}$ and $\\{b,d\\}$. A picture consists of disjoint arcs realizing the matching; equality is equality of the matching, so planar deformation that fixes the boundary does not change a diagram. If $m+n$ is odd, there are no such diagrams. Write $\\mathcal D(m,n)$ for this set and define the $R$-module\n\n$$M_R(m,n):=R^{(\\mathcal D(m,n))},$$\n\nthe free $R$-module on the set of diagrams ([[def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis]]). In particular, its diagram basis is empty when $m+n$ is odd.\n\nFor basis diagrams $D:m\\to n$ and $E:n\\to p$, stack $E$ above $D$ and identify the $n$ matching boundary points. The resulting finite graph has degree two at each identified point, where degree means the number of incident arcs. Each component is a path joining two exterior boundary points or a closed cycle. Remove every cycle and multiply the remaining exterior matching by $\\delta^c$, where $c$ is the number of removed cycles. Denote this output by $E\\circ D$ and extend the rule $R$-bilinearly to a map $M_R(n,p)\\times M_R(m,n)\\to M_R(m,p)$. A following result proves closure of these operations on the diagram modules and the category axioms.\n\nThe matching $1_n:n\\to n$ pairs each bottom point to the top point directly above it. Horizontal juxtaposition, with the left diagram placed first, defines $m\\otimes m'=m+m'$ on object labels and $D\\otimes D'$ on basis diagrams; extend it $R$-bilinearly to the modules. A following result proves that these operations give a strict monoidal category.\n\nFor $1\\le i<n$, let $d_i\\in M_R(n,n)$ be the diagram with a cap joining top points $i,i+1$, a cup joining bottom points $i,i+1$, and vertical through strands at every other position. Define $\\mathrm{TL}_n(R,\\delta)$ to be the $R$-module $M_R(n,n)$ with bilinear multiplication $xy:=x\\circ y$ and distinguished element $1_n$. A following result proves that it is a unital associative $R$-algebra and identifies it with the endomorphism algebra at object $n$.\n\nThe left factor is stacked above the right factor. The loop value is $\\delta$; no invertibility or nonzero condition on $\\delta$ is part of these data.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Using the conventions of [[def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category]], the standard\nTemperley–Lieb diagram for $e_i$ has a cap joining the two\nadjacent upper boundary points and a cup joining the corresponding lower\npoints, with all other strands vertical. Boundary points are numbered from\nleft to right and diagrams are composed by stacking the left factor above the\nright factor. In the spin representation each cap/cup contraction uses the\nunnormalized tensor $\\sqrt2\\,|\\omega\\rangle$; a closed loop therefore\ncontributes $2$. The proof above uses tensor contractions, not the diagram.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "The consumer links this exact carrier in its references or remarks; it supplies the declared local interface."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices",
          "carrier": "items/prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $s\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N=\\{0,\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\dots\\}$, put $N:=2s\\in\\mathbb N$ and let\n$V_s:=\\mathbb C^{N+1}$ carry the standard inner product\n$\\langle z,w\\rangle=\\sum_{k=0}^{N}z_k\\overline{w_k}$, linear in the first argument\n([[def-inner-product-space]]). Write $(e_m)_{-s\\le m\\le s}$ for the standard basis, indexed so that\n$e_m$ is the vector with entry $1$ in position $k=s-m$ and entry $0$ elsewhere; thus\n$\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=1$ if $m=n$ and $\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=0$ otherwise. Define $\\mathbb C$-linear maps\n$J_z,J_+,J_-\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V_s)$ by their values on this basis ([[def-linear-map]]):\n\n$$J_ze_m:=m\\,e_m,\\qquad J_+e_m:=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}\\,e_{m+1},\\qquad J_-e_m:=\\sqrt{(s+m)(s-m+1)}\\,e_{m-1},$$\n\nwhere $e_{s+1}:=0$ and $e_{-s-1}:=0$, and put\n\n$$J_x:=\\tfrac12(J_++J_-),\\qquad J_y:=\\tfrac1{2i}(J_+-J_-),\\qquad J^2:=J_x^2+J_y^2+J_z^2 .$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $J_z$, $J_x$ and $J_y$ are self-adjoint endomorphisms of $V_s$\n([[def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism]]), and $J_+^*=J_-$, $J_-^*=J_+$\n([[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]]);\n\n(b) on $V_s$ the commutators are\n\n$$[J_z,J_+]=J_+,\\qquad [J_z,J_-]=-J_-,\\qquad [J_+,J_-]=2J_z,\\qquad [J_x,J_y]=iJ_z,\\qquad [J_y,J_z]=iJ_x,\\qquad [J_z,J_x]=iJ_y ;$$\n\n(c) $J^2=J_-J_++J_z^2+J_z=J_+J_-+J_z^2-J_z=s(s+1)I$, and $[J^2,J_z]=[J^2,J_\\pm]=0$;\n\n(d) the matrix elements of the raising operator are\n$\\|J_+e_m\\|^2=(s-m)(s+m+1)$ and $\\|J_-e_m\\|^2=(s+m)(s-m+1)$, and\n$\\langle J_+e_m,e_{m+1}\\rangle=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}$, which is nonzero exactly when $m<s$;\n\n(e) for $s=\\tfrac12$, identify $V_{\\frac12}=\\mathbb C^2$ with the basis ordered as $(e_{\\frac12},e_{-\\frac12})$ and let\n\n$$\\sigma_x:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_y:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\ i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_z:=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nbe the Pauli matrices. Then $J_i=\\tfrac12\\sigma_i$ for $i=x,y,z$, $\\sigma_x^2=\\sigma_y^2=\\sigma_z^2=I$,\n$\\sigma_x\\sigma_y=i\\sigma_z$, $\\sigma_y\\sigma_z=i\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_z\\sigma_x=i\\sigma_y$, and consequently\n$\\sigma_i\\sigma_j+\\sigma_j\\sigma_i=2\\delta_{ij}I$ and $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I+i\\sum_k\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k$ for all $i,j$, where $\\delta_{ij}=1$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise, and $\\varepsilon_{ijk}=0$ when two of $i,j,k$ coincide, and otherwise $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$ is the sign of the permutation $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(i,j,k)$; the sum ranges over $k\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$. The same convention with indices $1,2,3$ uses the order-preserving relabelling $(1,2,3)=(x,y,z)$.\n\nAll statements are finite-dimensional linear algebra: no physical interpretation of $J_x,J_y,J_z$ as angular momentum, and no measurement postulate, is asserted here.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [[prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices]]: on $\\mathbb C^2$,\n$\\sigma_a\\sigma_b+\\sigma_b\\sigma_a=2\\delta_{ab}I$ for\n$a,b\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$; each displayed Pauli matrix is self-adjoint.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 supplies this interface in step(s) the stated assumptions."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers, let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with its standard\nHermitian inner product, and give $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$ its standard\ntensor-product inner product. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\}$ use the diagonal action\n$\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$. For a partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most\n$d$ rows, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding complex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N),\\qquad d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nEquip $S^\\lambda$ with any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip\n$M_\\lambda$ with the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F5] [[thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks]]: the place permutation action\nacts on $S^\\lambda$ and as identity on $M_\\lambda$, while the diagonal\n$U(2)$ action acts on the multiplicity factor.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft statement and complete proof inspected in this batch; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.2."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "N=2 on the four basis vectors |00>, |01>, |10>, |11>.",
          "result": "The adjacent swap is +1 on the symmetric triplet and −1 on the singlet; e=I−s=2Pω and e²=2e.",
          "verifies": "Two-site calculation in step 1.1."
        },
        {
          "check": "N=3 on ω₁₂⊗|0> and ω₁₂⊗|1>.",
          "result": "Both contractions give P₁₂P₂₃P₁₂=P₁₂/4, hence e₁e₂e₁=e₁.",
          "verifies": "Three-site contraction in step 2.1."
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-nrqmx-heisenberg-exchange-tl": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-spin-half-tensor-chain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\geq1$ be an integer. The finite spin-half tensor chain is the complex\ninner-product space\n$$\\mathcal H_N:=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$$\nwith the standard tensor-product inner product, linear in its first argument.\nFix the standard orthonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$ of $\\mathbb C^2$.\nLet $\\sigma^x,\\sigma^y,\\sigma^z$ be the Pauli matrices of the finite-spin\nmatrix supplier, and for $1\\leq k\\leq N$ define\n$$\\sigma_k^a:=I^{\\otimes(k-1)}\\otimes\\sigma^a\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-k)},\\qquad J_k^a:=\\tfrac12\\sigma_k^a\\quad(a\\in\\{x,y,z\\}).$$\nFor $1\\leq i<N$, define the adjacent swap $s_i$ on elementary tensors by\n$$s_i(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{i+1}\\otimes v_i\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N.$$\nWrite\n$$|\\omega\\rangle:=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(|0\\rangle\\otimes|1\\rangle-|1\\rangle\\otimes|0\\rangle),\\qquad P_i:=I^{\\otimes(i-1)}\\otimes P_\\omega\\otimes I^{\\otimes(N-i-1)},$$\nwhere $P_\\omega v:=\\langle v,\\omega\\rangle\\,\\omega$ on the adjacent two\nfactors. Also put $e_i:=I_{\\mathcal H_N}-s_i$ for $1\\leq i<N$. If $N=1$,\nthese bond-indexed families are empty.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition of finite-dimensional tensor spaces and\noperators; it makes no physical claim about a material, a particle species, or\nan adopted statistics sector.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N=\\{0,\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\dots\\}$, put $N:=2s\\in\\mathbb N$ and let\n$V_s:=\\mathbb C^{N+1}$ carry the standard inner product\n$\\langle z,w\\rangle=\\sum_{k=0}^{N}z_k\\overline{w_k}$, linear in the first argument\n([[def-inner-product-space]]). Write $(e_m)_{-s\\le m\\le s}$ for the standard basis, indexed so that\n$e_m$ is the vector with entry $1$ in position $k=s-m$ and entry $0$ elsewhere; thus\n$\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=1$ if $m=n$ and $\\langle e_m,e_n\\rangle=0$ otherwise. Define $\\mathbb C$-linear maps\n$J_z,J_+,J_-\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V_s)$ by their values on this basis ([[def-linear-map]]):\n\n$$J_ze_m:=m\\,e_m,\\qquad J_+e_m:=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}\\,e_{m+1},\\qquad J_-e_m:=\\sqrt{(s+m)(s-m+1)}\\,e_{m-1},$$\n\nwhere $e_{s+1}:=0$ and $e_{-s-1}:=0$, and put\n\n$$J_x:=\\tfrac12(J_++J_-),\\qquad J_y:=\\tfrac1{2i}(J_+-J_-),\\qquad J^2:=J_x^2+J_y^2+J_z^2 .$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $J_z$, $J_x$ and $J_y$ are self-adjoint endomorphisms of $V_s$\n([[def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism]]), and $J_+^*=J_-$, $J_-^*=J_+$\n([[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]]);\n\n(b) on $V_s$ the commutators are\n\n$$[J_z,J_+]=J_+,\\qquad [J_z,J_-]=-J_-,\\qquad [J_+,J_-]=2J_z,\\qquad [J_x,J_y]=iJ_z,\\qquad [J_y,J_z]=iJ_x,\\qquad [J_z,J_x]=iJ_y ;$$\n\n(c) $J^2=J_-J_++J_z^2+J_z=J_+J_-+J_z^2-J_z=s(s+1)I$, and $[J^2,J_z]=[J^2,J_\\pm]=0$;\n\n(d) the matrix elements of the raising operator are\n$\\|J_+e_m\\|^2=(s-m)(s+m+1)$ and $\\|J_-e_m\\|^2=(s+m)(s-m+1)$, and\n$\\langle J_+e_m,e_{m+1}\\rangle=\\sqrt{(s-m)(s+m+1)}$, which is nonzero exactly when $m<s$;\n\n(e) for $s=\\tfrac12$, identify $V_{\\frac12}=\\mathbb C^2$ with the basis ordered as $(e_{\\frac12},e_{-\\frac12})$ and let\n\n$$\\sigma_x:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_y:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\ i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_z:=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nbe the Pauli matrices. Then $J_i=\\tfrac12\\sigma_i$ for $i=x,y,z$, $\\sigma_x^2=\\sigma_y^2=\\sigma_z^2=I$,\n$\\sigma_x\\sigma_y=i\\sigma_z$, $\\sigma_y\\sigma_z=i\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_z\\sigma_x=i\\sigma_y$, and consequently\n$\\sigma_i\\sigma_j+\\sigma_j\\sigma_i=2\\delta_{ij}I$ and $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I+i\\sum_k\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k$ for all $i,j$, where $\\delta_{ij}=1$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise, and $\\varepsilon_{ijk}=0$ when two of $i,j,k$ coincide, and otherwise $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$ is the sign of the permutation $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(i,j,k)$; the sum ranges over $k\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$. The same convention with indices $1,2,3$ uses the order-preserving relabelling $(1,2,3)=(x,y,z)$.\n\nAll statements are finite-dimensional linear algebra: no physical interpretation of $J_x,J_y,J_z$ as angular momentum, and no measurement postulate, is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-spin-half-temperley-lieb",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\geq1$, $\\mathcal H_N=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$ with the standard\northonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$ on each factor, and for\n$1\\leq i<N$ define $s_i$ to swap factors $i,i+1$. Put\n$|\\omega\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$,\n$P_\\omega v=\\langle v,\\omega\\rangle\\omega$, let $P_i$ act as $P_\\omega$ on\nfactors $i,i+1$ and identity on all others, and set $e_i=I-s_i$. For every\nbond,\n$$e_i=I-s_i=2P_i,\\qquad e_i^*=e_i,\\qquad e_i^2=2e_i.$$\nFor $1\\leq i<N-1$,\n$$e_i e_{i+1}e_i=e_i,\\qquad e_{i+1}e_i e_{i+1}=e_{i+1},$$\nand $e_i e_j=e_j e_i$ when $|i-j|>1$. Consequently the assignment\n$u_i\\mapsto e_i$ defines a star-preserving algebra representation\n$$P_N(\\mathbb C,2)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}(\\mathcal H_N).$$\nFor $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most two rows, put\n$M_\\lambda=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,\\mathcal H_N)$ and use the\nunitary Schur–Weyl decomposition\n$\\mathcal H_N\\cong\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\,\\ell(\\lambda)\\le2}\nS^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$. Each $e_i$ acts on the Specht factor as\n$I-\\rho_\\lambda((i,i+1))$ and as identity on the multiplicity factor. No\nfaithfulness assertion is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers, let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with its standard\nHermitian inner product, and give $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$ its standard\ntensor-product inner product. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\}$ use the diagonal action\n$\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$. For a partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most\n$d$ rows, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding complex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N),\\qquad d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nEquip $S^\\lambda$ with any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip\n$M_\\lambda$ with the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $S_i^a=\\hbar\\sigma_i^a/2$, the factors $\\hbar^2$ cancel and\n$$\\hbar^{-2}\\sum_aS_i^aS_{i+1}^a=\\tfrac14\\sum_a\\sigma_i^a\\sigma_{i+1}^a.$$\nOn the ordered basis $|00\\rangle,|01\\rangle,|10\\rangle,|11\\rangle$, the\noperator $\\frac12(I+\\sum_a\\sigma_a\\otimes\\sigma_a)$ sends these four\nvectors respectively to $|00\\rangle,|10\\rangle,|01\\rangle,|11\\rangle$:\nfor the middle two, the Pauli sum is $2|10\\rangle-|01\\rangle$ and\n$2|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle$. This is exactly the swap, so\n$s_i=\\frac12(I+\\sum_a\\sigma_i^a\\sigma_{i+1}^a)$. Rearranging gives\n$\\frac12s_i-\\frac14I$, and substituting $e_i=I-s_i$ gives\n$\\frac14I-\\frac12e_i$. This proves the bond identity including its\nnormalization.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Each $e_i$ is self-adjoint by [F3], $I$ is self-adjoint, and every\n$\\mathcal J_i$ is real; hence their finite real linear combination $H$ is\nself-adjoint. By [F3] each $e_i$ lies in the represented Temperley–Lieb\nalgebra, so the displayed expression for $H$ is an element of that algebra's\nimage.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "An adjacent swap commutes with the diagonal action $U^{\\otimes N}$ for\nevery $U\\in U(2)$: on an elementary tensor, swapping the two entries after\napplying $U$ to each gives the same tensor as applying $U$ after the swap.\nThus $e_i=I-s_i$ and $H$ commute with that action. By [F4], the decomposition\nplaces the permutation operators on $S^\\lambda$ and the collective unitary\naction on $M_\\lambda$; therefore the formula for $H$ acts on $S^\\lambda$ and\nas identity on $M_\\lambda$. When $N=1$ there are no bonds and all displayed\nsums are zero.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
