    "thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $q_0:=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}\\in C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. There is a pointed\nhomeomorphism\n$$\\bigl(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0\\bigr)\\cong \\bigl(\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z, (0,1,[0])\\bigr),$$\nand\n$$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong(\\mathbb Z,+).$$\nUnder the isomorphism specified in the proof, the counterclockwise exchange\nloop\n$$\\gamma(t):=\\{(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t),(-\\cos\\pi t,-\\sin\\pi t)\\},\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq1,$$\nis a positive generator. The loop in which the relative direction makes one\nfull counterclockwise turn is $[\\gamma]^2$, so it represents twice the\ngenerator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-covering-map-and-evenly-covered-neighbourhoods",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **covering map** is a continuous surjection $p:E\\to B$ such that every $b\\in B$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which $p^{-1}(U)$ is a disjoint union of open sets $V_j$, called **sheets**, and each restriction $p|_{V_j}:V_j\\to U$ is a homeomorphism ([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]], [[def-disjoint-union-topology]]). Such a $U$ is **evenly covered**, and $p^{-1}(b)$ is the **fibre** over $b$. A covering is **trivial** when it is isomorphic over $B$ to a product projection $B\\times F\\to B$ with $F$ discrete.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-universal-covering-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **universal covering space** of $B$ is a covering map $p:\\widetilde B\\to B$ whose total space $\\widetilde B$ is simply connected ([[def-covering-map-and-evenly-covered-neighbourhoods]], [[def-simply-connected]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-deck-transformation-and-deck-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a covering $p:E\\to B$, a **deck transformation** is an isomorphism $h:E\\to E$ over $B$, so $p\\circ h=p$ ([[def-map-and-isomorphism-of-covering-spaces]]). Deck transformations form the **deck group** $\\operatorname{Deck}(p)$ under composition, and this group acts on $E$ by evaluation ([[def-group]], [[def-group-action]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the canonical copy of $\\mathbb Z$ inside $\\mathbb R$ fixed in\n[[lem-integer-part]]. For $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$, put\n\n$$x\\sim y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nThis is an equivalence relation. Indeed, $x-x=0\\in\\mathbb Z$; if\n$x-y\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $y-x=-(x-y)\\in\\mathbb Z$; and if\n$x-y,y-z\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)\\in\\mathbb Z$. The closure facts\nused here are part of the additive-group structure supplied by\n[[thm-int-comm-ring]], and the quotient-set construction is that of\n[[def-quotient-topology]].\n\nLet $[x]$ denote the equivalence class of $x$. Let\n$p:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the canonical projection,\n$p(x)=[x]$. Thus\n\n$$p(x)=p(y)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nLet $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ carry the quotient topology induced by $p$.\nThe circle is $S^1:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ with the quotient topology induced by $p(x)=[x]$ and basepoint $[0]$; moreover $p^{-1}([0])=\\mathbb Z$ and $p(x+n)=p(x)$ for every real $x$ and integer $n$.\n\nThe last assertions follow directly from the displayed fibre criterion:\n$p(x)=[0]$ exactly when $x\\in\\mathbb Z$, while\n$(x+n)-x=n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-simply-connected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A topological space $X$ is **simply connected** when it is nonempty and\npath-connected ([[def-path-connected]]) and, for every $x_0\\in X$, the group\n$\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ has exactly one element.\n\nRequiring every basepoint avoids presuming a change-of-basepoint theorem. For a\npath-connected space that later theorem shows that checking one basepoint is\nequivalent, but no such result is needed for this definition. The empty space\nis path-connected under the published convention, but it is not simply\nconnected here because nonemptiness is explicit.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-quotient-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The quotient topology.** Let $(X, \\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space\n([[def-topological-space]]), let $Y$ be a set and let $q : X \\to Y$ be a\nsurjection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). The **quotient topology** on\n$Y$ induced by $q$ is the final topology of the one-element family $(q)$\n([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]):\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_q \\;:=\\; \\{\\, V \\subseteq Y : q^{-1}[V] \\in \\mathcal{T} \\,\\} .$$\n\nThat this is a topology is discharged in [[def-initial-and-final-topology]],\nwhere every final topology is verified to satisfy (T1), (T2) and (T3). Dually,\n$C \\subseteq Y$ is closed in $\\mathcal{T}_q$ exactly when $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed\nin $X$, because $q^{-1}[Y \\setminus V] = X \\setminus q^{-1}[V]$.\n\n**Quotient map.** A surjection $q : X \\to Y$ between topological *spaces* is a\n**quotient map** (also *identification map*) when the topology of $Y$ is the\nquotient topology of $q$, that is when\n\n$$V \\text{ is open in } Y \\iff q^{-1}[V] \\text{ is open in } X \\qquad (V \\subseteq Y).$$\n\nThe implication from left to right is exactly continuity of $q$\n([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]] clause\n(b)), so a quotient map is a continuous surjection with the extra property that\nits topology is as *fine* as continuity permits. Equivalently, and this form is\nused as often: $C$ is closed in $Y$ if and only if $q^{-1}[C]$ is closed in $X$.\n\n**Saturated sets.** Let $q : X \\to Y$ be a surjection. A subset $A \\subseteq X$ is\n**saturated** with respect to $q$ when\n\n$$A \\;=\\; q^{-1}\\big[\\,q[A]\\,\\big] ,$$\n\nequivalently when $A$ is a union of fibres $q^{-1}[\\{y\\}]$, equivalently when\n$x \\in A$ and $q(x') = q(x)$ imply $x' \\in A$. The set $q^{-1}[q[A]]$ is the\n**saturation** of $A$, and it is the smallest saturated set containing $A$. The\nmap $V \\mapsto q^{-1}[V]$ is a bijection from $\\mathcal{P}(Y)$ onto the saturated\nsubsets of $X$, with inverse $A \\mapsto q[A]$, since $q$ is surjective; under it\nthe open sets of the quotient topology correspond exactly to the **saturated open\nsubsets of $X$**. That correspondence is the working description of the quotient\ntopology: to know the open sets of $Y$ is to know which open subsets of $X$ are\nsaturated.\n\n**Quotient by an equivalence relation.** Let $\\sim$ be an **equivalence relation**\non $X$, that is a relation that is reflexive ($x \\sim x$), symmetric\n($x \\sim x'$ implies $x' \\sim x$) and transitive ($x \\sim x'$ and $x' \\sim x''$\nimply $x \\sim x''$). Write $[x] := \\{\\, x' \\in X : x' \\sim x \\,\\}$ for the\n**equivalence class** of $x$; distinct classes are disjoint and their union is\n$X$. The **quotient set** is $X/\\!\\sim\\ := \\{\\, [x] : x \\in X \\,\\}$ and the\n**canonical projection** is\n\n$$\\pi : X \\to X/\\!\\sim, \\qquad \\pi(x) := [x] ,$$\n\nwhich is a surjection by construction. The set $X/\\!\\sim$ always carries the\nquotient topology of $\\pi$, and $(X/\\!\\sim,\\ \\mathcal{T}_\\pi)$ is called an\n**identification space**. A subset of $X$ is saturated for $\\pi$ exactly when it\nis a union of equivalence classes.\n\n**A convenient special case: collapsing a subset.** For $\\varnothing \\ne B \\subseteq X$\nlet $\\sim_B$ be the relation whose classes are $B$ itself and the singletons\n$\\{x\\}$ for $x \\notin B$; this is an equivalence relation, its classes being a\npartition of $X$. The resulting quotient is written $X/B$, and its canonical\nprojection is a surjection identifying all of $B$ to a single point and doing\nnothing else. Saturated sets for $\\sim_B$ are the sets $A$ with $A \\cap B \\in\n\\{\\varnothing, B\\}$.\n\n**Two conventions used throughout.** First, \"quotient map\" is a property of a map\n*together with the two topologies*, never of the map alone. Second, a quotient\ntopology is determined by $q$ together with the topology on its domain, and not by\nthe underlying pair of sets $(X,Y)$: two different surjections onto the same set\ncan give different topologies, the same surjection gives different topologies when\nits domain is retopologised, and where more than one is in play the map is named.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the pointed coordinate identification\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhich sends $q_0=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}$ to $(0,1,[0])$, to write\n$$X:=C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z.$$\nPut $\\widetilde X:=\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ and define\n$$p(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,[t])\\in X,\\qquad T_n(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,t+n)\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb Z).$$\nDefine $U(1):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$ under complex multiplication. Let\n$\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$ satisfy\n$\\chi(0)=1$ and $\\chi(m+n)=\\chi(m)\\chi(n)$ for all $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\nLet $\\mathbb Z$ act on\n$\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C$ by\n$$n\\cdot((c,\\rho,t),z):=((c,\\rho,t+n),\\chi(n)^{-1}z).$$\nThe homomorphism law for $\\chi$ makes this a group action, and its orbits\ndefine the quotient relation.\nDefine the **character quotient** and its projection by\n$$L_\\chi:=(\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C)/\\mathbb Z, \\qquad \\Pi_\\chi([\\widetilde x,z]):=p(\\widetilde x),$$\nwith the quotient topology on $L_\\chi$. Since $p(T_n\\widetilde x)=p(\\widetilde x)$,\nit induces a continuous map $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$. Once $p$ is known to be\na covering map, the **monodromy** of this quotient around a based loop means\nthe fiber map obtained by lifting the loop and keeping its complex coordinate\n$z$ constant along the lift.\n\nFor this item, a **unitary flat complex line bundle** over a space $X$ means a\ncontinuous surjection $\\Pi:L\\to X$ with local trivializations\n$\\Pi^{-1}(U)\\cong U\\times\\mathbb C$ over open sets $U$, whose changes of fiber\ncoordinate on overlaps are multiplication by functions $g:U\\cap V\\to U(1)$\nthat are locally constant (constant on some neighbourhood of each point).\nThe definition above names the quotient data; the following theorem verifies\nthat $L_\\chi$ has these line-bundle and flatness properties.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a topological space and let $x_0\\in X$. A **based loop at $x_0$**\nis a path $\\alpha:I\\to X$ with $\\alpha(0)=x_0=\\alpha(1)$\n([[def-path-connected]]). Two based loops are equivalent when they are\npath-homotopic relative to the endpoints. This is an equivalence relation by\n[[cor-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy-are-equivalence-relations]].\n\nThe **fundamental group set** of $X$ at $x_0$ is\n\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0):=\\{\\,[\\alpha]:\\alpha\\text{ is a based loop at }x_0\\,\\}.$$\n\nFor composable paths, write\n\n$$(\\alpha*\\beta)(s):=\\begin{cases}\\alpha(2s),&0\\leq s\\leq\\tfrac12,\\\\ \\beta(2s-1),&\\tfrac12\\leq s\\leq1.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe finite closed-pasting argument already carried out in\n[[def-path-connected]] shows that this is a path. The multiplication proposed\non loop classes is\n\n$$[\\alpha][\\beta]:=[\\alpha*\\beta].$$\n\n**Order convention.** The product $[\\alpha][\\beta]$ traverses $\\alpha$ first\nand $\\beta$ second. Every product on this page uses this convention.\n\nThe constant loop at $x_0$ is denoted $c_{x_0}$, and the reversed loop is\n$\\bar\\alpha(s):=\\alpha(1-s)$. The next theorem proves that multiplication is\nindependent of representatives and that $[c_{x_0}]$ and $[\\bar\\alpha]$ are the\nidentity and inverse required by the group axioms.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a covering, let $\\alpha:I\\to B$ be a path, and let $e_0\\in E$ satisfy $p(e_0)=\\alpha(0)$. There is a unique path $\\widetilde\\alpha:I\\to E$ with $\\widetilde\\alpha(0)=e_0$ and $p\\circ\\widetilde\\alpha=\\alpha$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The projection $p(c,\\rho,t)=(c,\\rho,[t])$ is continuous and surjective.\nFor any $[t_0]\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, choose an open interval\n$I=(a,b)$ around a representative of $[t_0]$ with $b-a<1$. Its image is an\nopen arc because its full preimage $\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}(I+n)$ is open\nby [F5, F7]; the translates are disjoint by $b-a<1$. On each sheet $I+n$,\nthe quotient map $t\\mapsto[t]$ is a homeomorphism onto that arc: it is a\ncontinuous bijection and maps open subsets to open subsets by the same\nsaturation check. Taking products with the other two coordinate factors\ngives evenly covered open neighborhoods in $X$, so [F2] makes $p$ a covering\nmap.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F4], a deck transformation $D$ satisfies $pD=p$. Hence $D$ fixes\nthe $\\mathbb R^2$ and $(0,\\infty)$ coordinates and has last coordinate\n$t+k(c,\\rho,t)$ for an integer-valued continuous function $k$, by [F5].\nThe space $\\widetilde X$ is connected because it is convex: its radius\ncoordinate stays positive on every line segment. Therefore $k$ is constant;\nthus $D=T_n$ for some\n$n\\in\\mathbb Z$. Conversely each $T_n$ preserves $p$ and is a homeomorphism,\nso these are exactly the deck transformations.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The space $\\widetilde X$ is nonempty and convex in $\\mathbb R^4$ because\nthe radius coordinate remains positive along every line segment. For any\nbasepoint $x_0\\in\\widetilde X$ and based loop $\\alpha$, the straight-line\nhomotopy $H_s(t)=(1-s)\\alpha(t)+s x_0$ remains in $\\widetilde X$ and fixes\nboth endpoints. Thus every based loop from [F9] is null-homotopic, so [F6]\nmakes $\\widetilde X$ simply connected. By [F3], $p$ is a universal covering\nmap.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The action in [F8] is a group action: composing the $m$ and $n$ actions\nadds the shifts and multiplies the fiber coordinate by\n$\\chi(m)^{-1}\\chi(n)^{-1}=\\chi(m+n)^{-1}$. The action is free because\n$T_n\\widetilde x=\\widetilde x$ implies $n=0$. If $U$ is an evenly covered\nneighborhood as in step 1.1 and $V$ is one chosen sheet, every orbit over\n$U$ has exactly one representative in $V\\times\\mathbb C$. The quotient\ntopology therefore identifies $\\Pi_\\chi^{-1}(U)$ homeomorphically with\n$U\\times\\mathbb C$: the restriction from $V\\times\\mathbb C$ is bijective,\nand it is open because the saturation of an open subset is the union of its\ntranslates under the homeomorphisms in [F8]. The fiber coordinate is the\nunique representative's complex coordinate. This gives a local trivialization\nwith one-dimensional complex fibers.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On an overlap, two chosen sheets differ locally by a fixed translation\n$T_n$, because the sheets of a covering are disjoint and indexed by the\ndiscrete integers. The corresponding fiber coordinate changes by\n$z\\mapsto\\chi(n)z$ or its inverse, according to the direction of the chart\nchange. Since $|\\chi(n)|=1$ and $n$ is locally constant, these transition\nfunctions are locally constant $U(1)$-valued functions. By [F8], this is a\nunitary flat complex line bundle.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let a based loop have a lift from $\\widetilde x$ to $T_n\\widetilde x$;\nthe path-lifting theorem [F10] supplies a unique lift from every prescribed\nstarting point. Keeping $z$ constant along this lift gives endpoint class\n$[T_n\\widetilde x,z]$. The defining equivalence in [F8] gives\n$[T_n\\widetilde x,z]=[\\widetilde x,\\chi(n)z]$, so monodromy is multiplication\nby $\\chi(n)$. The explicit positive exchange path has lifted angle coordinate\n$t\\mapsto t$ from $0$ to $1$, while the full relative turn has $t\\mapsto2t$\nfrom $0$ to $2$, by the homeomorphism in [F1]. Thus their monodromies are\n$\\chi(1)$ and $\\chi(2)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
