    "thm-nrqmx-two-dimensional-exchange-holonomy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $q_0:=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}\\in C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. There is a pointed\nhomeomorphism\n$$\\bigl(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0\\bigr)\\cong \\bigl(\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z, (0,1,[0])\\bigr),$$\nand\n$$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong(\\mathbb Z,+).$$\nUnder the isomorphism specified in the proof, the counterclockwise exchange\nloop\n$$\\gamma(t):=\\{(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t),(-\\cos\\pi t,-\\sin\\pi t)\\},\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq1,$$\nis a positive generator. The loop in which the relative direction makes one\nfull counterclockwise turn is $[\\gamma]^2$, so it represents twice the\ngenerator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the canonical copy of $\\mathbb Z$ inside $\\mathbb R$ fixed in\n[[lem-integer-part]]. For $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$, put\n\n$$x\\sim y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nThis is an equivalence relation. Indeed, $x-x=0\\in\\mathbb Z$; if\n$x-y\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $y-x=-(x-y)\\in\\mathbb Z$; and if\n$x-y,y-z\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)\\in\\mathbb Z$. The closure facts\nused here are part of the additive-group structure supplied by\n[[thm-int-comm-ring]], and the quotient-set construction is that of\n[[def-quotient-topology]].\n\nLet $[x]$ denote the equivalence class of $x$. Let\n$p:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the canonical projection,\n$p(x)=[x]$. Thus\n\n$$p(x)=p(y)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nLet $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ carry the quotient topology induced by $p$.\nThe circle is $S^1:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ with the quotient topology induced by $p(x)=[x]$ and basepoint $[0]$; moreover $p^{-1}([0])=\\mathbb Z$ and $p(x+n)=p(x)$ for every real $x$ and integer $n$.\n\nThe last assertions follow directly from the displayed fibre criterion:\n$p(x)=[0]$ exactly when $x\\in\\mathbb Z$, while\n$(x+n)-x=n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n\n$$C=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$$\n\nwith the Euclidean subspace topology. The function\n\n$$h:\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow C,\\qquad h([t])=(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t),$$\n\nis a homeomorphism. Thus $[t]\\mapsto(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t)$ is a homeomorphism from $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ to the unit circle and sends $[0]$ to $(1,0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Form the polynomial ring $\\mathbb R[x]$\n([[def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and define the **complex\nnumbers** by the quotient ring\n$$\\mathbb C:=\\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)$$\n([[def-quotient-ring]]). Write $a$ for the constant class $a+(x^2+1)$ and set\n$$i:=x+(x^2+1).$$\nThus $i^2=-1$ in the quotient. The constant-class map\n$\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ is the specified real map; its injectivity and the\nfield structure are proved in [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]], after which\n$\\mathbb C/\\mathbb R$ is a field extension in the sense of\n[[def-field-extension-generated-subfields-and-simple-extension]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ has unique coordinates $z=a+bi$\n([[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]). Define\n$$\\operatorname{Re}z=a,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z=b,\\qquad \\overline z=a-bi,$$\nand define its **modulus** by\n$$|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.$$\nThe real numbers are least-upper-bound complete\n([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), so the nonnegative square root exists and\nis unique by [[thm-of-square-roots]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Complex conjugation is a real-field automorphism satisfying\n$$\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{\\overline z}=z.$$\nFor every $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$,\n$$z\\overline z=|z|^2,\\qquad |z|\\ge0,\\qquad |z|=0\\Longleftrightarrow z=0,$$\n$$|zw|=|z||w|,\\qquad |z+w|\\le |z|+|w|.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\mathbb C=\\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)$ is a field containing the embedded copy of\n$\\mathbb R$. Every complex number has a unique form $a+bi$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$,\nand\n$$ (a+bi)+(u+vi)=(a+u)+(b+v)i, $$\n$$ (a+bi)(u+vi)=(au-bv)+(av+bu)i. $$\nIf $a+bi\\ne0$, then\n$$ (a+bi)^{-1}=\\frac{a-bi}{a^2+b^2}. $$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers, let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with its standard\nHermitian inner product, and give $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$ its standard\ntensor-product inner product. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\}$ use the diagonal action\n$\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$. For a partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most\n$d$ rows, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding complex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N),\\qquad d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nEquip $S^\\lambda$ with any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip\n$M_\\lambda$ with the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-column-antisymmetrizer-polytabloid-and-specht-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge 0$, let $\\lambda\\vdash n$, and let $t$ be a $\\lambda$-tableau\n([[def-partition-young-diagram-and-conjugate-partition]],\n[[def-young-tableau-standard-tableau-and-shape]]). Use the left action of\n$S_n$ on tableaux, tabloids, and $M^\\lambda$\n([[def-young-subgroup-tabloid-and-permutation-module]]). For each\n$\\gamma\\in C_t$, let $\\operatorname{sgn}(\\gamma)$ be its inversion sign in\n$S_n$. The order-preserving relabelling\n$\\iota_n:\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\to\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\}$, $i\\mapsto i-1$, carries each\ninversion pair $(i,j)$ bijectively to $(i-1,j-1)$; thus this sign is exactly\nthe published inversion sign on the finite ordinal\n([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). For $n=0$, both groups are\ntrivial and the sign is $1$.\n\nThe **column antisymmetrizer**, **polytabloid**, and **Specht space** are\n$$\\kappa_t:=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in C_t}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\gamma)\\gamma\\quad\\in\\mathbb C[S_n],\\qquad e_t:=\\kappa_t\\cdot\\{t\\}\\quad\\in M^\\lambda,\\qquad S^\\lambda:=\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{e_s:s\\text{ is a }\\lambda\\text{-tableau}\\}.$$\nThe elements of the sum are in the finite subgroup $C_t$, and each acts by the\ndeclared left action on the tabloid basis, so these are well-defined finite\nexpressions.\n\nFor every tableau, $C_t\\cap R_t=\\{1\\}$: a permutation in both stabilizers\npreserves the row and column of each entry, and each row-column intersection\ncontains at most one node. Thus $\\gamma\\{t\\}$ are distinct as $\\gamma$ ranges\nover $C_t$, and the coefficient of $\\{t\\}$ in $e_t$ is $1$. In particular,\n$e_t\\ne0$. When $n=0$, the empty tableau has $C_t=\\{1\\}$, so\n$\\kappa_t=1$, $e_t=\\{\\varnothing\\}$, and $S^\\varnothing=\\mathbb C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every character $\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$, the coordinate covering\n$p:\\widetilde X\\to X$ and quotient $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$ defined by the\ncharacter-quotient construction have the following properties:\n\n1. $p$ is a universal covering map and its deck transformations are exactly\n   $T_n(c,\\rho,t)=(c,\\rho,t+n)$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n2. $\\Pi_\\chi$ is a unitary flat complex line bundle, meaning its local\n   transition phases are locally constant.\n3. Parallel transport defined by keeping the lifted fiber coordinate fixed\n   has monodromy $\\chi(n)$ on a based loop whose lift ends at $T_n\\widetilde x$.\n\nIn particular, a positive planar exchange has monodromy $\\chi(1)$ and a full\nrelative winding has monodromy $\\chi(2)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $g=[\\gamma]$. By [F1], every element of the fundamental group is\nuniquely $ng$ for $n\\in\\mathbb Z$. A homomorphism $\\chi$ is therefore\ndetermined by $z:=\\chi(g)$: the homomorphism law gives\n$\\chi(ng)=z^n$, including negative $n$ because $z^{-1}$ is the group inverse\nin $U(1)$. Conversely, for any $z\\in U(1)$ this formula preserves addition\nof integer exponents and defines a homomorphism. By [F3], the pair\n$(\\operatorname{Re}z,\\operatorname{Im}z)$ lies on the unit circle. By [F2]\nthere is a unique $[t]\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ for which\n$z=\\cos(2\\pi t)+i\\sin(2\\pi t)$. Put $\\theta:=2\\pi t$ modulo $2\\pi$.\nRepeated application of [F4] gives\n$z^n=\\cos(n\\theta)+i\\sin(n\\theta)$ for $n\\geq0$; for $n<0$, use\n$z^{-1}=\\overline z=\\cos(-\\theta)+i\\sin(-\\theta)$ by [F5], and the same\ninduction.\nThis proves the full character classification and the displayed formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Write $S_2=\\{e,\\tau\\}$ with $\\tau=(12)$ and $\\tau^2=e$. The\npermutation quotient $q:\\mathbb Z\\to S_2$, $q(n)=\\tau^n$, is onto and has\nkernel $2\\mathbb Z$. A character factors through that quotient exactly when\n$z^2=1$, where\n$z=\\chi(g)$. By [F6], this forces $z=1$ or $z=-1$. The parameterization\n[F2] is unique modulo $2\\pi$; [F7] identifies these points with angles\n$0$ and $\\pi$, respectively. The resulting generator values are $+1$ and\n$-1$. For shape $(2)$ the unique tableau has trivial\ncolumn stabilizer, so its column antisymmetrizer is the identity and the\none-dimensional Specht module is trivial; for shape $(1,1)$ the column\nantisymmetrizer is $1-(12)$, whose image is the sign line. This direct use of\nthe Specht definition [F9] identifies the two factors.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], one full relative turn represents $2g$. The homomorphism law\nthen gives $\\chi(2g)=z^2=\\cos(2\\theta)+i\\sin(2\\theta)=e^{2i\\theta}$ by\n[F4].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "On $(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes2}$ the swap fixes $|00\\rangle$ and sends\n$|\\omega\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ to $-|\\omega\\rangle$.\nThus both exchange eigenspaces are nonzero. They are the $+1$ and $-1$\nSpecht factors in the Schur–Weyl decomposition [F8, F9], so the two permutation\ncharacters occur algebraically. This establishes occurrence only; it does\nnot select a physical statistics sector.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F10] to $\\chi_\\theta$. The positive exchange is the loop class\n$g$ from [F1], whose lift shifts the normalized angle coordinate by $1$;\ntherefore its monodromy is $\\chi_\\theta(1)=e^{i\\theta}$. A full relative\nturn shifts by $2$, so its monodromy is $\\chi_\\theta(2)=e^{2i\\theta}$ by\nstep 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F10",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 includes n=0 and the homomorphism identity χ(0)=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At θ=0, step 1.1 gives z=1 and step 2.1 gives full-winding phase 1; step 3.1 gives exchange monodromy 1."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At θ=π, step 1.2 gives z=−1 and step 2.1 gives full-winding phase 1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 treats exactly z²=1 and uses the field property to obtain z=±1."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 identifies the unique angular representatives 0 and π modulo 2π."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 uses the unique class [t] supplied by the circle parameterization."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2: factorization through S₂ forces z²=1 and hence θ≡0 or π modulo 2π."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2: when z=±1, the character is trivial on the kernel 2Z and factors through S₂."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-configuration-space",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-configuration-space.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Give $\\mathbb R^4$ its Euclidean topology, whose basic open sets are the\nEuclidean open balls, and write a point as an ordered pair $(x,y)$ with\n$x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$. The **ordered configuration space** is the subspace\n$$F_2(\\mathbb R^2):=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2:x\\ne y\\}$$\nwith the subspace topology. The transposition acts by $(x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)$.\nThe **unordered configuration space of two distinct planar points** is\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2):=F_2(\\mathbb R^2)/\\bigl((x,y)\\sim(y,x)\\bigr)$$\nwith the quotient topology of the canonical projection\n$q:F_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\to C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. We write its points as unordered\nsets $\\{x,y\\}$; equality in this set means equality up to transposition.\nThe diagonal $x=y$ is excluded, so collisions are not points of this space.\n\nThe topology on each finite Cartesian coordinate space used below is its\nEuclidean topology; on a finite product it is equivalently the topology with\nbasis the products of open sets in the factors.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft definition and exact interface inspected; proof is not applicable.",
          "consumer_use": "The consumer links this exact carrier in its references or remarks; it supplies the declared local interface."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $q_0:=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}\\in C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. There is a pointed\nhomeomorphism\n$$\\bigl(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0\\bigr)\\cong \\bigl(\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z, (0,1,[0])\\bigr),$$\nand\n$$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong(\\mathbb Z,+).$$\nUnder the isomorphism specified in the proof, the counterclockwise exchange\nloop\n$$\\gamma(t):=\\{(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t),(-\\cos\\pi t,-\\sin\\pi t)\\},\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq1,$$\nis a positive generator. The loop in which the relative direction makes one\nfull counterclockwise turn is $[\\gamma]^2$, so it represents twice the\ngenerator.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [[lem-nrqmx-planar-two-particle-config-fundamental-group]]:\n$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong\\mathbb Z$ with $[\\gamma]$ corresponding\nto $1$; one full relative turn corresponds to $2$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft statement and complete proof inspected in this batch; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1, 1.2, 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers, let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with its standard\nHermitian inner product, and give $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$ its standard\ntensor-product inner product. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\}$ use the diagonal action\n$\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$. For a partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most\n$d$ rows, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding complex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N),\\qquad d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nEquip $S^\\lambda$ with any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip\n$M_\\lambda$ with the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F8] [[thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks]]: on\n$(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes2}$ the transposition acts on the Specht factor and as\nidentity on the multiplicity factor; the collective $U(2)$ action acts on\nthe multiplicity factor.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft statement and complete proof inspected in this batch; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 supplies this interface in step(s) the stated assumptions."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers",
          "carrier": "items/def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the canonical copy of $\\mathbb Z$ inside $\\mathbb R$ fixed in\n[[lem-integer-part]]. For $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$, put\n\n$$x\\sim y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nThis is an equivalence relation. Indeed, $x-x=0\\in\\mathbb Z$; if\n$x-y\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $y-x=-(x-y)\\in\\mathbb Z$; and if\n$x-y,y-z\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)\\in\\mathbb Z$. The closure facts\nused here are part of the additive-group structure supplied by\n[[thm-int-comm-ring]], and the quotient-set construction is that of\n[[def-quotient-topology]].\n\nLet $[x]$ denote the equivalence class of $x$. Let\n$p:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the canonical projection,\n$p(x)=[x]$. Thus\n\n$$p(x)=p(y)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nLet $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ carry the quotient topology induced by $p$.\nThe circle is $S^1:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ with the quotient topology induced by $p(x)=[x]$ and basepoint $[0]$; moreover $p^{-1}([0])=\\mathbb Z$ and $p(x+n)=p(x)$ for every real $x$ and integer $n$.\n\nThe last assertions follow directly from the displayed fibre criterion:\n$p(x)=[0]$ exactly when $x\\in\\mathbb Z$, while\n$(x+n)-x=n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [[def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers]],\n[[thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle]]: every\npoint of the unit circle is $(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t)$ for a unique\n$[t]\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle",
          "carrier": "items/thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let\n\n$$C=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$$\n\nwith the Euclidean subspace topology. The function\n\n$$h:\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow C,\\qquad h([t])=(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t),$$\n\nis a homeomorphism. Thus $[t]\\mapsto(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t)$ is a homeomorphism from $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ to the unit circle and sends $[0]$ to $(1,0)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [[def-circle-as-real-line-mod-integers]],\n[[thm-real-line-mod-integers-is-homeomorphic-to-the-unit-circle]]: every\npoint of the unit circle is $(\\cos 2\\pi t,\\sin 2\\pi t)$ for a unique\n$[t]\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1, 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic",
          "carrier": "items/def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Form the polynomial ring $\\mathbb R[x]$\n([[def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and define the **complex\nnumbers** by the quotient ring\n$$\\mathbb C:=\\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)$$\n([[def-quotient-ring]]). Write $a$ for the constant class $a+(x^2+1)$ and set\n$$i:=x+(x^2+1).$$\nThus $i^2=-1$ in the quotient. The constant-class map\n$\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ is the specified real map; its injectivity and the\nfield structure are proved in [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]], after which\n$\\mathbb C/\\mathbb R$ is a field extension in the sense of\n[[def-field-extension-generated-subfields-and-simple-extension]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]],\n[[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]],\n[[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]: every $z=a+ib$ has unique real\ncoordinates and $|z|^2=a^2+b^2$, so $|z|=1$ exactly when $(a,b)$ is on the\nEuclidean unit circle.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
          "carrier": "items/def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ has unique coordinates $z=a+bi$\n([[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]). Define\n$$\\operatorname{Re}z=a,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z=b,\\qquad \\overline z=a-bi,$$\nand define its **modulus** by\n$$|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.$$\nThe real numbers are least-upper-bound complete\n([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), so the nonnegative square root exists and\nis unique by [[thm-of-square-roots]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]],\n[[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]],\n[[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]: every $z=a+ib$ has unique real\ncoordinates and $|z|^2=a^2+b^2$, so $|z|=1$ exactly when $(a,b)$ is on the\nEuclidean unit circle.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field",
          "carrier": "items/thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "$\\mathbb C=\\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)$ is a field containing the embedded copy of\n$\\mathbb R$. Every complex number has a unique form $a+bi$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$,\nand\n$$ (a+bi)+(u+vi)=(a+u)+(b+v)i, $$\n$$ (a+bi)(u+vi)=(au-bv)+(av+bu)i. $$\nIf $a+bi\\ne0$, then\n$$ (a+bi)^{-1}=\\frac{a-bi}{a^2+b^2}. $$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]: $\\mathbb C$ is a field, so\n$z^2=1$ implies $z=1$ or $z=-1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
          "carrier": "items/lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Complex conjugation is a real-field automorphism satisfying\n$$\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{\\overline z}=z.$$\nFor every $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$,\n$$z\\overline z=|z|^2,\\qquad |z|\\ge0,\\qquad |z|=0\\Longleftrightarrow z=0,$$\n$$|zw|=|z||w|,\\qquad |z+w|\\le |z|+|w|.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]],\n[[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]],\n[[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]: every $z=a+ib$ has unique real\ncoordinates and $|z|^2=a^2+b^2$, so $|z|=1$ exactly when $(a,b)$ is on the\nEuclidean unit circle.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "carrier": "items/thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]]: the sine and cosine addition\nformulas hold for all real inputs.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1, 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "carrier": "items/cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F5] [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]: for real $x$,\n$\\cos(-x)=\\cos x$ and $\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 supplies this interface in step(s) 1.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "carrier": "items/thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F7] [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]]: $\\cos0=1$, $\\sin0=0$,\n$\\cos\\pi=-1$, and $\\sin\\pi=0$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published statement and complete proof inspected; no new independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-column-antisymmetrizer-polytabloid-and-specht-module",
          "carrier": "items/def-column-antisymmetrizer-polytabloid-and-specht-module.md",
          "status": "Current published supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n\\ge 0$, let $\\lambda\\vdash n$, and let $t$ be a $\\lambda$-tableau\n([[def-partition-young-diagram-and-conjugate-partition]],\n[[def-young-tableau-standard-tableau-and-shape]]). Use the left action of\n$S_n$ on tableaux, tabloids, and $M^\\lambda$\n([[def-young-subgroup-tabloid-and-permutation-module]]). For each\n$\\gamma\\in C_t$, let $\\operatorname{sgn}(\\gamma)$ be its inversion sign in\n$S_n$. The order-preserving relabelling\n$\\iota_n:\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\to\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\}$, $i\\mapsto i-1$, carries each\ninversion pair $(i,j)$ bijectively to $(i-1,j-1)$; thus this sign is exactly\nthe published inversion sign on the finite ordinal\n([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). For $n=0$, both groups are\ntrivial and the sign is $1$.\n\nThe **column antisymmetrizer**, **polytabloid**, and **Specht space** are\n$$\\kappa_t:=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in C_t}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\gamma)\\gamma\\quad\\in\\mathbb C[S_n],\\qquad e_t:=\\kappa_t\\cdot\\{t\\}\\quad\\in M^\\lambda,\\qquad S^\\lambda:=\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{e_s:s\\text{ is a }\\lambda\\text{-tableau}\\}.$$\nThe elements of the sum are in the finite subgroup $C_t$, and each acts by the\ndeclared left action on the tabloid basis, so these are well-defined finite\nexpressions.\n\nFor every tableau, $C_t\\cap R_t=\\{1\\}$: a permutation in both stabilizers\npreserves the row and column of each entry, and each row-column intersection\ncontains at most one node. Thus $\\gamma\\{t\\}$ are distinct as $\\gamma$ ranges\nover $C_t$, and the coefficient of $\\{t\\}$ in $e_t$ is $1$. In particular,\n$e_t\\ne0$. When $n=0$, the empty tableau has $C_t=\\{1\\}$, so\n$\\kappa_t=1$, $e_t=\\{\\varnothing\\}$, and $S^\\varnothing=\\mathbb C$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F9] [[def-column-antisymmetrizer-polytabloid-and-specht-module]]: the\n$S_2$-Specht module of shape $(2)$ is the one-dimensional trivial module;\nthe Specht module of shape $(1,1)$ is the one-dimensional sign module, by\napplying the column antisymmetrizer to the unique tableau of each shape.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; proof is not applicable; the current interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F9 supplies this interface in step(s) 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the pointed coordinate identification\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhich sends $q_0=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}$ to $(0,1,[0])$, to write\n$$X:=C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z.$$\nPut $\\widetilde X:=\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ and define\n$$p(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,[t])\\in X,\\qquad T_n(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,t+n)\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb Z).$$\nDefine $U(1):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$ under complex multiplication. Let\n$\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$ satisfy\n$\\chi(0)=1$ and $\\chi(m+n)=\\chi(m)\\chi(n)$ for all $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\nLet $\\mathbb Z$ act on\n$\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C$ by\n$$n\\cdot((c,\\rho,t),z):=((c,\\rho,t+n),\\chi(n)^{-1}z).$$\nThe homomorphism law for $\\chi$ makes this a group action, and its orbits\ndefine the quotient relation.\nDefine the **character quotient** and its projection by\n$$L_\\chi:=(\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C)/\\mathbb Z, \\qquad \\Pi_\\chi([\\widetilde x,z]):=p(\\widetilde x),$$\nwith the quotient topology on $L_\\chi$. Since $p(T_n\\widetilde x)=p(\\widetilde x)$,\nit induces a continuous map $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$. Once $p$ is known to be\na covering map, the **monodromy** of this quotient around a based loop means\nthe fiber map obtained by lifting the loop and keeping its complex coordinate\n$z$ constant along the lift.\n\nFor this item, a **unitary flat complex line bundle** over a space $X$ means a\ncontinuous surjection $\\Pi:L\\to X$ with local trivializations\n$\\Pi^{-1}(U)\\cong U\\times\\mathbb C$ over open sets $U$, whose changes of fiber\ncoordinate on overlaps are multiplication by functions $g:U\\cap V\\to U(1)$\nthat are locally constant (constant on some neighbourhood of each point).\nThe definition above names the quotient data; the following theorem verifies\nthat $L_\\chi$ has these line-bundle and flatness properties.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[[def-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle]]",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft definition and exact interface inspected; proof is not applicable.",
          "consumer_use": "The consumer links this exact carrier in its references or remarks; it supplies the declared local interface."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For every character $\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$, the coordinate covering\n$p:\\widetilde X\\to X$ and quotient $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$ defined by the\ncharacter-quotient construction have the following properties:\n\n1. $p$ is a universal covering map and its deck transformations are exactly\n   $T_n(c,\\rho,t)=(c,\\rho,t+n)$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n2. $\\Pi_\\chi$ is a unitary flat complex line bundle, meaning its local\n   transition phases are locally constant.\n3. Parallel transport defined by keeping the lifted fiber coordinate fixed\n   has monodromy $\\chi(n)$ on a based loop whose lift ends at $T_n\\widetilde x$.\n\nIn particular, a positive planar exchange has monodromy $\\chi(1)$ and a full\nrelative winding has monodromy $\\chi(2)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F10] [[thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle]]: for any character\n$\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$, the associated quotient is a flat unitary complex\nline bundle, and a loop whose lift ends at deck translate $n$ has monodromy\n$\\chi(n)$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step-1 draft statement and complete proof inspected in this batch; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F10 supplies this interface in step(s) 3.1."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "θ=0.",
          "result": "χ(n)=1 for every n, and both exchange and full-winding phases are 1.",
          "verifies": "Trivial character and full winding in steps 1.1 and 2.1."
        },
        {
          "check": "θ=π.",
